Here are a few ways to write a JavaScript function that removes duplicates from an array, depending on whether you want to preserve the original order or not.

### 1. Using `Set` (Most concise and modern)

The `Set` object in JavaScript only stores unique values. This is the most idiomatic and often the fastest way to remove duplicates.

```javascript
/**
 * Removes duplicate elements from an array, preserving the order of first appearance.
 * @param {Array<any>} arr The input array which may contain duplicates.
 * @returns {Array<any>} A new array containing only unique elements.
 */
function removeDuplicatesUsingSet(arr) {
  // Create a new Set from the array (which automatically removes duplicates)
  // Then convert the Set back into an Array using the spread operator (...).
  return [...new Set(arr)];
}

// Example Usage:
const arrayWithDuplicates = [1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4, 5, 1];
const uniqueArray = removeDuplicatesUsingSet(arrayWithDuplicates);

console.log(uniqueArray); // Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

### 2. Using `filter` and `indexOf` (Preserves order)

This method iterates through the array and keeps an element only if its first occurrence's index matches the current index. This is useful if you need to support older environments that don't fully support `Set`, or if you prefer a more explicit loop structure.

```javascript
/**
 * Removes duplicate elements from an array while preserving the original order.
 * @param {Array<any>} arr The input array which may contain duplicates.
 * @returns {Array<any>} A new array containing only unique elements.
 */
function removeDuplicatesUsingFilter(arr) {
  return arr.filter((item, index) => {
    // Keep the item only if the first time we see it is at the current index.
    return arr.indexOf(item) === index;
  });
}

